Database Administration: MySQL Backup and Restore Operations

1. Overview & Objectives

Database backup and recovery procedures form the cornerstone of enterprise data governance, ensuring data availability, business continuity, and resilience against hardware failure, corruption, or human error.

Key Concepts
Backup Strategies:
Logical Backups: Export human-readable SQL statements (e.g., CREATE TABLE, INSERT). Highly portable across platforms and MySQL versions, though slower for multi-terabyte datasets.
Physical Backups: Consist of raw files storing database contents (data files, index files, transaction logs). Faster to restore for massive databases (e.g., using MySQL Enterprise Backup or Percona XtraBackup).
Full vs. Incremental Backups: Full backups capture the complete dataset at a given time. Incremental backups capture only the delta (changes) generated since the last full or incremental execution.
MySQL Dump Utilities:
mysqldump: The standard client utility used to perform logical backups by producing a set of SQL statements required to recreate schema objects and repopulate data.
mysqlpump / MySQL Shell Dump: Modern multi-threaded logical export tools designed for higher parallel throughput.
Point-In-Time Recovery (PITR):
Achieved by combining a baseline full logical/physical backup with Binary Logs (binlog).
By replaying binary log events up to a specific timestamp or transaction GTID, an administrator can recover the database state to the exact second prior to an incident (e.g., an accidental DROP TABLE).


2. Step 1: Exporting / Backing Up a Database (mysqldump)

The mysqldump client command exports database objects to a flat .sql text file. Below are standard command structures, flags, and operational variations using a target database named sales_db.

Base Command Syntax
mysqldump -u [username] -p[password] [options] [database_name] > [backup_file.sql]

Options Overview
Flag / Option

Description

-u, --user

Specifies the MySQL user account.

-p, --password

Prompts for or passes the account password.

--single-transaction

Sets transaction isolation to REPEATABLE READ before dumping; ensures transactional consistency for InnoDB without locking the database.

--quick

Forces mysqldump to retrieve rows from the server one at a time rather than buffering entire row sets in memory.

--routines

Includes stored procedures and functions in the output.

--triggers

Includes triggers for dumped tables (enabled by default).

--no-data

Dumps only the database structure (schema DDL).

--no-create-info

Dumps only data (INSERT statements), omitting table DDL.



Practical Dump Execution Examples
Full Database Backup (sales_db)
Generates a complete backup containing structure, data, procedures, and triggers using consistent non-blocking reads for InnoDB tables:


mysqldump -u root -p \

  --single-transaction \

  --quick \

  --routines \

  --triggers \

  sales_db > sales_db_full_backup.sql

Structural Dump Only (Schema DDL)
Captures object definitions (CREATE TABLE, CREATE PROCEDURE) without table rows:h
mysqldump -u root -p
--no-data
--routines
--triggers
sales_db > sales_db_schema_only.sql




#### Data-Only Dump

Captures row data using `INSERT` statements while suppressing table creation commands:


```bash

mysqldump -u root -p \

  --no-create-info \

  sales_db > sales_db_data_only.sql

Backing Up Multiple Databases or All Databases
To capture multiple specific databases or the entire database instance:


# Selected databases

mysqldump -u root -p --single-transaction --databases sales_db inventory_db > multi_db_backup.sql


# All databases instance-wide

mysqldump -u root -p --single-transaction --all-databases > full_server_backup.sql

