3. Step 2: Restoring a Database

Restoring a MySQL database from a .sql logical dump involves executing the SQL statements contained in the file against a running MySQL server.

Method A: Command Line Interface (CLI)
Before importing, ensure the target database exists on the server. If it does not exist, create it via the MySQL client shell.l
-- Connect to MySQL and create destination database
CREATE DATABASE IF NOT EXISTS sales_db;




Execute the import using standard input redirection via command line:


```bash

mysql -u root -p sales_db < sales_db_full_backup.sql



If restoring a backup generated with --all-databases or --databases, explicit target database names are not required as the dump includes USE database; directives:


mysql -u root -p < full_server_backup.sql




Method B: MySQL Workbench (GUI)
To restore a .sql file using the graphical environment:


Launch MySQL Workbench and establish a connection to the target server instance.
In the Navigator pane on the left, navigate to Management $\rightarrow$ Data Import/Restore.
Select Import from Self-Contained File and click ... to browse to your .sql file location.
Under Default Target Schema, select sales_db (or click New to create a target schema name).
In the Dump Structure and Data section, select Dump Structure and Data, Dump Data Only, or Dump Structure Only depending on requirement.
Click the Start Import tab at the bottom right and click Start Import. Monitor the progress bar and log window for completion.


4. Step 3: Automating and Managing Backups

Automating backup schedules ensures operational reliability and reduces manual overhead.

Automated Backup Script (backup_sales.sh)
Create a shell script on Linux systems to execute daily automated backups with compressed outputs and date stp naming:h
#!/usr/bin/env bash

MySQL Automated Backup Script

TIMESTAMP=$(date +"%Y%m%d_%H%M%S")
BACKUP_DIR="/var/backups/mysql"
DB_NAME="sales_db"
RETENTION_DAYS=7

Ensure backup directory exists

mkdir -p ${BACKUP_DIR}

Execute dump with default option file credentials

mysqldump --defaults-extra-file=/etc/mysql/conf.d/backup.cnf
--single-transaction
--quick
${DB_NAME} | gzip > ${BACKUP_DIR}/${DB_NAME}_${TIMESTAMP}.sql.gz

Check exit status

if [ $? -eq 0 ]; then
echo "[SUCCESS] Backup generated: ${BACKUP_DIR}/${DB_NAME}_${TIMESTAMP}.sql.gz"
else
echo "[ERROR] Backup failed for database: ${DB_NAME}" >&2
fi

Prune backups older than retention window

find ${BACKUP_DIR} -name "${DB_NAME}_*.sql.gz" -type f -mtime +${RETENTION_DAYS} -delete




